New hypotheses and measurements
-------------------------------
1. Explicit C register declarations might influence MWCC's global allocation
   priorities. The is_type_8, target_state, and combined variants were byte-identical
   to the 97.49789% baseline and rejected.
2. Retaining a signed-short rank temporary may defer its sign extension differently
   from an immediately promoted integer local. Changing only that local to s32
   produced 98.73418%. fn_801CEB2C's s16 return declaration is unchanged. Every possible
   returned s16 value is exactly representable in s32, so the shift, byte truncation,
   signed subtraction, and floating conversion have the same values as before.
3. Follow-up mask scheduling, guard nesting, scalar types, condition forms, early
   initialization, and removal of existing parameter aliases did not improve the
   promoted-rank result. The complete journal records each source patch against the
   accepted base, raw assigned-object build output/exit code, and raw objdiff JSON
   for every successful build. Its summary is derived from that journal.

The sole retained C change is s16 rank -> s32 rank. It is a distinct independent
translation unit; its existing NonMatching registration and split remain unchanged.
No compiler flags, externalization policy, verification gates, neighboring functions,
runtime code, README, or global progress were edited. No agents were delegated.
Some exploratory variants changed the compiler-local constant name and failed the
existing @120 externalization step. Those failures are preserved verbatim and the
variants were discarded; no policy workaround was introduced. One exploratory
variant failed C89 declaration placement and its corrected variant was separately
recorded. The final retained candidate builds successfully under the existing policy.

